\title{\textbf{Margin Scarcity as the Portability Predictor}\\[0.4em]
\large Certified forward-pass screens for block transplantation, and the
refutation of weight-space distance as a predictor of damage}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{1 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Two candidate predictors of the damage caused by transplanting a block of layers
from one fine-tuned network into another are analysed and compared: a \emph{norm
route}, in which portability is inferred from the weight-space distance between
the two copies of the block via a Lipschitz estimate, and a \emph{margin route},
in which portability is inferred from the fraction of evaluation positions whose
score vector carries no margin certificate. We prove that the margin route
yields an unconditional upper bound on the measured post-transplant decision
disagreement --- the \emph{damage fraction} --- computable from a single forward
pass, with no transplant performed; and that the norm route does not.
Specifically: (i) the damage fraction never exceeds the margin-uncertified
fraction, so a forward-pass statistic below a threshold $\tau$ certifies damage
below $\tau$; (ii) the norm route is sound only as a sufficient condition, and as
a predictor it is refuted --- for every $0 < d < D$ there exist two block pairs
over identical features, one at entrywise weight distance $D$ with damage $0$ and
one at distance $d$ with damage $1$, whence any damage bound depending on the
weight distance alone must equal the trivial bound $1$ everywhere; (iii) across a
family of blocks, if the margin screen overshoots the damage by at most $\eta$
then the empirical covariance of predictor and damage is at least
$\Var(\dam) - \tfrac{\eta}{2}\sqrt{\Var(\dam)}$, hence strictly positive once the
damage spread exceeds $\eta/2$; while an explicit two-block family gives
covariance $+1/4$ for the margin statistic and $-(D-d)/4$ for the weight distance
on the \emph{same} data. We further supply two strictly cheaper relaxations: a
R\'enyi-2 (collision-mass) diffuseness fraction that lower-bounds the margin
statistic, making entropic diffuseness a certified obstruction to certification;
and a reverse-Markov bound $\damage \le (G-\mu)/(G-2\eps)$ from only a gap cap
$G$ and a mean gap $\mu$, which is attained. Instantiated at a measured two-arm
transplant experiment (damages $0.4557$ and $0.1615$), the theory certifies
positive cross-block covariance for any screen slack $\eta < 0.2942$ and produces
a directly falsifiable prediction: the donor tail's mean top-1 gap cannot exceed
$2.8673$ nats at $G=5$, $\eps = 0.16$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} margin certificate, transplant damage, Lipschitz
bound, R\'enyi-2 entropy, collision mass, reverse Markov inequality, empirical
covariance, model merging.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Two networks share an architecture and a common ancestor but have been fine-tuned
on different data. A \emph{block transplant} replaces a contiguous group of
layers in the host network with the corresponding group from the donor, keeping
everything else fixed, and then asks how much of the host's behaviour survives.
This operation underlies model merging, layer splicing, adapter grafting, and a
range of interpretability probes.

The operation is cheap to \emph{perform} and expensive to \emph{evaluate}:
measuring how much damage a graft does requires assembling the hybrid and running
it over a held-out corpus, for every candidate block. What practitioners want
instead is a \emph{screen}: a statistic computable before any surgery, whose value
predicts, or at minimum bounds, the damage.

The folklore screen is weight-space distance. It is motivated by a genuine
estimate --- a small weight perturbation moves logits by a Lipschitz-bounded
amount, and a decision with a large enough margin survives a bounded logit
perturbation --- and it is very widely used. This paper asks whether that
motivation supports the use, and concludes that it does not.

\subsection{Contributions}

We work with a decision-level notion of damage: the fraction of held-out
positions at which the hybrid's argmax differs from the reference's argmax.
Against this target:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The norm route is sound but only as a sufficient condition}
  (Theorem~\ref{thm:norm-sound}). Under an entrywise weight-distance bound
  $\delta$, a feature bound $B$, and width $k$, every logit moves by at most
  $k\delta B$; if the host's top-1 gap exceeds $2k\delta B$ everywhere, the
  damage is exactly zero.
\item \textbf{The margin screen} (Theorem~\ref{thm:screen}). The damage fraction
  is bounded above by the margin-uncertified fraction --- a statistic of the
  donor's own forward pass and a drift budget. Hence portability screening at any
  threshold (Corollary~\ref{cor:screening}).
\item \textbf{Refutation of the norm route as a predictor}
  (Theorems~\ref{thm:nonmono} and~\ref{thm:no-norm-bound}). Damage is not
  monotone in weight distance, in the strongest possible sense; and any damage
  bound that is a function of the weight distance alone is identically $\ge 1$.
\item \textbf{Sharp boundaries of the margin screen}
  (Theorems~\ref{thm:conservative} and~\ref{thm:attained}). The screen is
  strictly one-sided and it is attained, so no universal multiplicative
  improvement exists.
\item \textbf{Cross-block correlation} (Theorems~\ref{thm:corr}
  and~\ref{thm:poscorr}), and an explicit family on which margin and distance
  have opposite covariance signs (Theorem~\ref{thm:opposite}).
\item \textbf{Two strictly cheaper relaxations.} A R\'enyi-2 diffuseness fraction
  lower-bounding the margin statistic (Theorems~\ref{thm:bridge}--\ref{thm:renyi}),
  and a two-scalar reverse-Markov damage bound
  (Theorems~\ref{thm:revmarkov}--\ref{thm:twoscalar}), with an attainment result
  and a falsifiable numerical consequence (Theorem~\ref{thm:falsifiable}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setup and definitions}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set of \emph{positions} (tokens of a
held-out corpus, or validation examples), and $m \ge 1$ is the number of decision
classes.

\begin{definition}[Strict top]
For $f : \{1,\dots,m\} \to \mathbb{R}$ and an index $j$, we say $j$ is the
\emph{strict top} of $f$ if $f(i) < f(j)$ for every $i \ne j$. A strict top is
unique when it exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Damage fraction]
Given decision maps $f, g : \Omega \to \{1,\dots,m\}$, the \emph{damage fraction}
is
\[
\damage(f,g) \;=\; \frac{\#\{x \in \Omega : f(x) \ne g(x)\}}{\#\Omega}\;\in[0,1].
\]
Writing $\agree(f,g)$ for the complementary fraction, $\damage = 1 - \agree$.
\end{definition}

In the transplant scenario, $g = d$ is the \emph{reference} decision and
$f = d_H$ is the \emph{hybrid} decision after the graft, so $\damage(d_H,d)$ is
the measured post-transplant disagreement.

\begin{definition}[Margin certificate]\label{def:mc}
Let $u,v : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^m$ be score (logit) fields, $d : \Omega \to
\{1,\dots,m\}$ a decision map, and $\eps \ge 0$ a \emph{drift budget}. A position
$x$ is \emph{margin-certified at level $\eps$} if
\[
\text{(gap)}\quad u(x)_{d(x)} - u(x)_j > 2\eps \ \ \forall j \ne d(x),
\qquad
\text{(drift)}\quad |u(x)_j - v(x)_j| \le \eps \ \ \forall j .
\]
The \emph{uncertified set} $U_\eps$ is the set of positions that are not
certified, and the \emph{margin-uncertified fraction}, or \emph{margin scarcity},
is $\unc(u,v,d,\eps) = \# U_\eps / \#\Omega$.
\end{definition}

The certificate is local: the gap condition is read off the donor's own forward
pass, and the drift condition is a hypothesis about how far the hybrid's scores
can move. Nothing in it requires the hybrid to be built.

\begin{lemma}[Margin stability]\label{lem:stab}
If $f(j) - f(i) > 2\eps$ for all $i \ne j$ and $|f(i) - h(i)| \le \eps$ for all
$i$, then $j$ is the strict top of $h$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $i \ne j$: $h(j) \ge f(j) - \eps > f(i) + \eps \ge h(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Certified positions are safe]\label{rem:safe}
Immediately from Lemma~\ref{lem:stab}: if $x$ is margin-certified and $d_H(x)$ is
the strict top of $v(x)$, then $d_H(x) = d(x)$. Contrapositively, every
\emph{damaged} position is uncertified:
$\{x : d_H(x) \ne d(x)\} \subseteq U_\eps$.
\end{remark}

\section{The norm route, stated honestly}

\begin{definition}[Linear block]
For weights $W \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times k}$ and features
$\feat : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^k$, the block's logits are
$\logit_W(x)_j = \sum_{i=1}^{k} W_{ji}\,\feat(x)_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Lipschitz / norm bound]\label{lem:lip}
If $|(W_A)_{ji} - (W_B)_{ji}| \le \delta$ for all $j,i$ and
$|\feat(x)_i| \le B$ for all $x,i$, then for every $x$ and $j$,
\[
\bigl|\logit_{W_A}(x)_j - \logit_{W_B}(x)_j\bigr| \;\le\; k\,\delta\,B.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The difference equals $\sum_i \bigl((W_A)_{ji} - (W_B)_{ji}\bigr)\feat(x)_i$.
Apply the triangle inequality termwise; each term is at most $\delta B$; there are
$k$ terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The norm route is sound]\label{thm:norm-sound}
In the setting of Lemma~\ref{lem:lip}, suppose $d_A$ satisfies, for all $x$ and
all $j \ne d_A(x)$,
\[
2\,(k\delta B) \;<\; \logit_{W_A}(x)_{d_A(x)} - \logit_{W_A}(x)_j,
\]
and $d_B(x)$ is the strict top of $\logit_{W_B}(x)$ for every $x$. Then
$d_B(x) = d_A(x)$ for every $x$; consequently $\damage(d_B,d_A) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $x$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:lip} the perturbation from $\logit_{W_A}(x)$ to
$\logit_{W_B}(x)$ is bounded by $k\delta B$ coordinatewise, and by hypothesis the
gap of $d_A(x)$ exceeds $2k\delta B$. Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} makes $d_A(x)$ the
strict top of $\logit_{W_B}(x)$; uniqueness of strict tops gives
$d_B(x) = d_A(x)$. The disagreement set is empty.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:norm-sound} is exactly what the norm route buys: a
\emph{sufficient condition}. Two features of the bound deserve emphasis, because
they are what Section~\ref{sec:refute} exploits: the bound involves $\delta$
\emph{and} $B$ \emph{and} $k$, so the weight distance alone does not determine it;
and the bound is a worst case over feature directions, whereas the actual damage
depends on the drift's component along the directions the features occupy.

\section{The margin route: a forward-pass screen}

\begin{theorem}[The margin screen]\label{thm:screen}
Let $u,v : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^m$, $d : \Omega \to \{1,\dots,m\}$, $\eps \ge 0$,
and let $d_H(x)$ be the strict top of $v(x)$ for every $x$. Then
\[
\damage(d_H, d) \;\le\; \unc(u,v,d,\eps).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Remark~\ref{rem:safe}, $\{x : d_H(x) \ne d(x)\} \subseteq U_\eps$; divide
cardinalities by $\#\Omega$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Portability screening]\label{cor:screening}
If $\unc(u,v,d,\eps) \le \tau$ then $\damage(d_H,d) \le \tau$.
\end{corollary}

This is the operational statement. The left-hand side requires one forward pass of
the donor over the held-out set, a histogram of top-1 gaps, and a drift budget
$\eps$; the right-hand side requires building the hybrid and evaluating it.

\begin{theorem}[Saturation on a measured arm]\label{thm:sat}
If the measured agreement of a transplant is $\agree(d_H,d) = 0.5443$, then
$\damage(d_H,d) = 0.4557$ and $\unc(u,v,d,\eps) \ge 0.4557$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first claim is $\damage = 1 - \agree$; the second is Theorem~\ref{thm:screen}
applied to the first.
\end{proof}

On this arm the screen is \emph{exactly saturated}: the certified lower bound on
margin scarcity coincides numerically with the measured damage, so the screen has
zero slack there.

\section{Weight-space distance is not a predictor}\label{sec:refute}

We now show the norm route cannot be upgraded from a sufficient condition to a
predictor. The construction lives in $m = k = 2$ and uses a \emph{dead feature
direction}.

\begin{definition}[Dead-direction features]
Let $\feat(x) = (1,0)$ for every $x \in \Omega$. Then $\logit_W(x)_j = W_{j1}$ for
every $x$: the second weight column is invisible.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Damage is not monotone in weight distance]\label{thm:nonmono}
For every $0 < d < D$ there exist $W_A, W_B, W_A', W_B' \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times2}$
and decision maps such that, over the dead-direction features:
each of the four blocks has a strict top at every position;
$|(W_A)_{ji} - (W_B)_{ji}| \le D$ with equality in the entry $(1,2)$;
$|(W_A')_{ji} - (W_B')_{ji}| \le d$ with equality in the entry $(1,1)$;
and $\damage(d_B,d_A) = 0$ while $\damage(d_B',d_A') = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take
\[
W_A = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\ 0 & 0\end{pmatrix},\quad
W_B = \begin{pmatrix}1 & D\\ 0 & 0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
W_A' = \begin{pmatrix}d/2 & 0\\ -d/2 & 0\end{pmatrix},\quad
W_B' = \begin{pmatrix}-d/2 & 0\\ d/2 & 0\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Over the dead-direction features, $\logit_{W_A}(x) = \logit_{W_B}(x) = (1,0)$, so
both decide class $1$ and the disagreement set is empty. Meanwhile
$\logit_{W_A'}(x) = (d/2,-d/2)$ decides class $1$ while
$\logit_{W_B'}(x) = (-d/2,d/2)$ decides class $2$: the disagreement set is all of
$\Omega$. The entrywise distance claims are immediate.
\end{proof}

The first pair may be at arbitrarily large weight distance $D$ and yet transplants
perfectly; the second may be at arbitrarily small distance $d$ and yet destroys
every decision.

\begin{theorem}[No norm-only damage bound exists]\label{thm:no-norm-bound}
Suppose $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfies: for every $\delta$, every pair
$W_A,W_B \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times2}$ with $|(W_A)_{ji} - (W_B)_{ji}| \le \delta$,
every feature field, and every pair of strict-top decision maps, one has
$\damage(d_B,d_A) \le g(\delta)$. Then $g(\delta) \ge 1$ for every $\delta > 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $\delta > 0$ and instantiate the hypothesis at the live-direction pair of
Theorem~\ref{thm:nonmono} with $d = \delta$: the entrywise distance is
$\le \delta$, both blocks have strict tops, and the damage is $1$.
\end{proof}

Since the damage fraction always lies in $[0,1]$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:no-norm-bound} says the only norm-only bound is the trivial one.
Note what it does \emph{not} say: it does not contradict
Theorem~\ref{thm:norm-sound}, whose hypothesis involves the gap and hence the
feature scale. The missing ingredient is the geometry of the drift relative to the
live feature directions --- the same weight perturbation is harmless in a dead
direction and fatal in a live one.

\section{The boundary of the margin screen}

\begin{theorem}[The screen is only sufficient]\label{thm:conservative}
There exist $u,v,d,d_H$ with $d_H(x)$ the strict top of $v(x)$ such that
$\unc(u,v,d,1) = 1$ and $\damage(d_H,d) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $m=2$, $u(x)=v(x)=(1,0)$, $d \equiv d_H \equiv 1$. The gap is $1$, which
fails to exceed $2\eps = 2$, so no position is certified and $\unc = 1$; but
$d_H = d$ identically, so the damage is $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The screen is attained]\label{thm:attained}
There exist $u,v,d,d_H$ as above with $\unc(u,v,d,1) = 1$ and
$\damage(d_H,d) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $u(x)=(1,0)$, $v(x)=(0,1)$, $d \equiv 1$, $d_H \equiv 2$. Again the gap $1$
fails to exceed $2$, so $\unc = 1$; and the decisions disagree everywhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
There is no constant $c<1$ with $\damage \le c\cdot\unc$ for all configurations:
Theorem~\ref{thm:screen} is optimal among bounds of that shape.
\end{corollary}

\section{Across blocks: correlation}

In deployment one ranks a stack of $L$ blocks rather than screening one.

\begin{definition}[Family statistics]
For $f,g : \{1,\dots,L\} \to \mathbb{R}$,
\[
\overline{f} = \frac{1}{L}\sum_b f(b), \quad
\cov(f,g) = \frac{1}{L}\sum_b (f(b)-\overline{f})(g(b)-\overline{g}), \quad
\Var(f) = \cov(f,f).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Elementary properties]
$\Var(f) = \frac{1}{L}\sum_b (f(b)-\overline f)^2 \ge 0$; $\cov$ is symmetric and
additive in each argument; and $\Var(f) = \frac{1}{L}\sum_b f(b)^2 -
\overline{f}^{\,2}$ when $L>0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Cauchy--Schwarz for the empirical covariance]\label{lem:cs}
$\cov(f,g)^2 \le \Var(f)\Var(g)$, equivalently
$|\cov(f,g)| \le \sqrt{\Var(f)}\sqrt{\Var(g)}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Apply the discrete Cauchy--Schwarz inequality to the centred vectors
$(f(b)-\overline f)_b$ and $(g(b)-\overline g)_b$, then divide by $L^2$; take
square roots for the absolute-value form.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Popoviciu-type variance bound]\label{lem:pop}
If $0 \le e(b) \le \eta$ for all $b$, then $\Var(e) \le \eta^2/4$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $0 \le e(b) \le \eta$ we get $e(b)^2 \le \eta\,e(b)$, hence
$\frac{1}{L}\sum_b e(b)^2 \le \eta\overline e$. Therefore
$\Var(e) \le \eta\overline e - \overline e^{\,2}
= \eta^2/4 - (\overline e - \eta/2)^2 \le \eta^2/4$.
\end{proof}

Let $\dam(b)$ be the measured damage of block $b$ and $\pred(b)$ its
margin-uncertified fraction; Theorem~\ref{thm:screen} gives
$\dam(b) \le \pred(b)$. Say the screen is \emph{tight to $\eta$} if
$\pred(b) \le \dam(b) + \eta$ for every $b$.

\begin{theorem}[Correlation lower bound for the margin screen]\label{thm:corr}
If $\dam(b) \le \pred(b) \le \dam(b)+\eta$ for all $b$, then
\[
\cov(\pred,\dam) \;\ge\; \Var(\dam) - \frac{\eta}{2}\sqrt{\Var(\dam)}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $L=0$ both sides vanish. Otherwise the two hypotheses at any single block force
$\eta \ge 0$. Set $e(b) = \pred(b)-\dam(b)$, so $\pred = \dam + e$ with
$0 \le e \le \eta$ pointwise. Additivity of covariance gives
$\cov(\pred,\dam) = \Var(\dam) + \cov(e,\dam)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:pop},
$\sqrt{\Var(e)} \le \eta/2$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:cs},
\[
\cov(e,\dam) \ge -|\cov(e,\dam)| \ge -\sqrt{\Var(e)}\sqrt{\Var(\dam)}
\ge -\frac{\eta}{2}\sqrt{\Var(\dam)} . \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positive correlation]\label{thm:poscorr}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:corr} with $L \ge 1$, if
$\sqrt{\Var(\dam)} > \eta/2$ then $\cov(\pred,\dam) > 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $s = \sqrt{\Var(\dam)} \ge 0$. Theorem~\ref{thm:corr} gives
$\cov \ge s^2 - \tfrac{\eta}{2}s = s(s - \tfrac{\eta}{2}) > 0$ since
$s > \eta/2 \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Numerical instantiation]
For the two measured arms, $\dam = (0.4557,\,0.1615)$: the mean is $0.3086$, the
deviations are $\pm 0.1471$, so $\Var(\dam) = 0.021638\ldots$ and
$\sqrt{\Var(\dam)} = 0.14710$. Theorem~\ref{thm:poscorr} therefore certifies
$\cov > 0$ for any screen slack $\eta < 0.29420$; Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} shows the
tail arm has slack $\eta = 0$ exactly.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}[Two-block covariance in closed form]\label{lem:cov2}
For $L=2$, $\cov\bigl((a,b),(c,e)\bigr) = \tfrac14 (a-b)(c-e)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The centred vectors are $\pm\tfrac12(a-b)$ and $\pm\tfrac12(c-e)$ with matching
signs; averaging the two equal products gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The two extremal blocks]\label{lem:blocks}
Over dead-direction features and at drift $\eps = 0$: the dead-direction pair
$W_A, W_B$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:nonmono} has margin-uncertified fraction $0$ and
damage $0$; the live-direction pair $W_A', W_B'$ has margin-uncertified fraction
$1$ and damage $1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Dead-direction block: both weight matrices give logits $(1,0)$ everywhere, so the
gap is $1 > 0 = 2\eps$ and the drift is $0 \le \eps$; every position is certified
and $\unc = 0$; the decisions coincide, so the damage is $0$. Live-direction
block: the donor logits are $(d/2,-d/2)$ and the hybrid logits $(-d/2,d/2)$, so
the drift in the first coordinate is $d > 0 = \eps$; the drift clause fails at
every position, $\unc = 1$; and the decisions differ everywhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The margin predicts, the norm anti-predicts]\label{thm:opposite}
For every $0 < d < D$ there is a two-block family over identical features and
architecture --- the two blocks of Lemma~\ref{lem:blocks} --- with weight
distances $(D,d)$ and damages $(0,1)$, such that
\[
\cov(\text{margin statistic},\,\text{damage}) = \frac14 > 0,
\qquad
\cov(\text{weight distance},\,\text{damage}) = -\frac{D-d}{4} < 0 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:blocks} the margin statistics are $(0,1)$ and the damages are
$(0,1)$; Lemma~\ref{lem:cov2} gives $\tfrac14(0-1)(0-1) = \tfrac14$. The distances
are $(D,d)$; Lemma~\ref{lem:cov2} gives $\tfrac14(D-d)(0-1) = -\tfrac{D-d}{4}$,
negative since $D>d$.
\end{proof}

On one and the same measurement, the cheap forward-pass statistic orders the
blocks by portability correctly, while the weight-space distance orders them
exactly backwards. Weight distance is not a loose predictor of transplant damage;
on this family it is an \emph{anti}-predictor.

\section{Cheaper: R\'enyi-2 diffuseness as an obstruction}

\begin{definition}[Collision mass and R\'enyi-2 entropy]
For $p \in \mathbb{R}^n$, the \emph{collision mass} is $C(p) = \sum_k p_k^2$ and
the \emph{R\'enyi-2 entropy} is $H_2(p) = -\log C(p)$. Small collision mass means
the vector is spread out, or \emph{diffuse}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Diffuse set and fraction]
For a nonnegative score field $u$ and $\eps \ge 0$, the \emph{diffuse set} is
$\{x \in \Omega : C(u(x)) \le 4\eps^2\}$, and $\diff(u,\eps)$ is its normalised
cardinality.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The bridge: diffuse implies uncertified]\label{thm:bridge}
Let $m>1$, $\eps \ge 0$, and suppose $u(x)_k \ge 0$ for all $k$. If
$C(u(x)) \le 4\eps^2$ then $x$ is \emph{not} margin-certified at level $\eps$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $x$ were certified. Since $m>1$ there is $j \ne d(x)$, and the gap clause
gives $u(x)_{d(x)} - u(x)_j > 2\eps$; as $u(x)_j \ge 0$ this forces
$u(x)_{d(x)} > 2\eps$. But for a nonnegative vector the largest entry satisfies
$u(x)_{d(x)} \le \sqrt{\sum_k u(x)_k^2} = \sqrt{C(u(x))} \le 2\eps$. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fractional form]\label{thm:difffrac}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} at every position,
$\diff(u,\eps) \le \unc(u,v,d,\eps)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} is the set inclusion
$\{\text{diffuse}\} \subseteq U_\eps$; divide cardinalities by $\#\Omega$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy form of the criterion]\label{thm:renyi}
For $p$ with $C(p) > 0$ and $\eps > 0$,
$H_2(p) \ge 2\log\frac{1}{2\eps} \iff C(p) \le 4\eps^2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$2\log\frac{1}{2\eps} = -\log\bigl((2\eps)^2\bigr) = -\log(4\eps^2)$, so the left
side reads $\log C(p) \le \log(4\eps^2)$, which by strict monotonicity of $\log$
on the positives is $C(p) \le 4\eps^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The obstruction sandwich]
For a block with nonnegative score vectors, $m>1$, $\eps\ge0$, and $d_H(x)$ the
strict top of $v(x)$:
\[
\diff(u,\eps) \le \unc(u,v,d,\eps)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\damage(d_H,d) \le \unc(u,v,d,\eps).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Concentration is necessary for certification]
If $\unc(u,v,d,\eps) \le \tau$ then $\diff(u,\eps) \le \tau$: a block certified
portable below $\tau$ by the margin route is necessarily $\tau$-concentrated.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The sandwich cannot be closed]\label{thm:sandwich-open}
There exist $u,v,d,d_H$ and $\eps>0$ with $d_H$ a strict top of $v$ such that
$\diff(u,\eps) = 1$, $\unc(u,v,d,\eps) = 1$ and $\damage(d_H,d) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $m=2$, $\eps=1$, $u(x)=v(x)=(1,0)$, $d \equiv d_H \equiv 1$. Then
$C(u(x)) = 1 \le 4 = 4\eps^2$, so $\diff = 1$; the gap $1$ does not exceed
$2\eps=2$, so $\unc = 1$; and $d_H = d$, so the damage is $0$.
\end{proof}

Both inequalities of the sandwich point \emph{the same way} into $\unc$, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich-open} shows this is not an artefact: diffuseness bounds
the certificate, not the damage.

\section{Cheapest: two scalars bound the damage}

\begin{definition}[Gap surrogate, low-margin set]
Let $g : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfy $g(x) \le u(x)_{d(x)} - u(x)_j$ for all
$x$ and all $j \ne d(x)$. The \emph{low-margin set} at drift $\eps$ is
$\{x : g(x) \le 2\eps\}$, with normalised cardinality $\lmf(g,\eps)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Uncertified positions are low-margin]\label{lem:lowmargin}
If also $|u(x)_j - v(x)_j| \le \eps$ for all $x,j$, then
$U_\eps \subseteq \{x : g(x) \le 2\eps\}$, hence $\unc \le \lmf(g,\eps)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Contrapositive: if $g(x) > 2\eps$ then
$u(x)_{d(x)} - u(x)_j \ge g(x) > 2\eps$ for every $j \ne d(x)$, so the gap clause
holds; the drift clause holds by hypothesis; so $x$ is certified.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reverse Markov for margins]\label{thm:revmarkov}
Let $g(x) \le G$ for all $x$, let $2\eps < G$, and suppose
$\mu\,\#\Omega \le \sum_x g(x)$. Then
$\lmf(g,\eps) \le \dfrac{G-\mu}{G-2\eps}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be the low-margin set and $T$ its complement, $|S|+|T| = N := \#\Omega$.
Then
\[
\mu N \le \sum_x g(x) = \sum_{S} g + \sum_{T} g
\le |S|(2\eps) + |T|G = |S|(2\eps) + (N-|S|)G,
\]
so $|S|(G-2\eps) \le (G-\mu)N$; divide by $N(G-2\eps) > 0$.
\end{proof}

Intuitively: a high average under a hard ceiling leaves little mass available for
stragglers below $2\eps$.

\begin{theorem}[Two forward-pass scalars bound the damage]\label{thm:twoscalar}
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:lowmargin} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:revmarkov}, with $d_H(x)$ the strict top of $v(x)$,
\[
\damage(d_H,d) \;\le\; \frac{G-\mu}{G-2\eps}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Chain Theorem~\ref{thm:screen}, Lemma~\ref{lem:lowmargin} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:revmarkov}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The bound is attained]
For $2\eps < G$, the two-position family with gap surrogate values $(2\eps, G)$
has mean gap $(2\eps+G)/2$, and both the reverse-Markov bound and the low-margin
fraction equal $1/2$:
\[
\lmf = \frac12, \qquad
\frac{G - \frac{2\eps+G}{2}}{G-2\eps} = \frac{\frac{G-2\eps}{2}}{G-2\eps}
= \frac12 .
\]
\end{theorem}

Hence no improvement of Theorem~\ref{thm:revmarkov} as a function of
$(G,\mu,\eps)$ alone is possible.

\begin{theorem}[A falsifiable numerical consequence]\label{thm:falsifiable}
If a transplant's measured damage is $0.4557$ and its gap statistics at drift
$\eps$ are a cap $G$ and a mean $\mu$ with $2\eps < G$, then
$\mu \le G - 0.4557\,(G-2\eps)$. At $G = 5$ nats and $\eps = 0.16$ this caps the
donor's mean top-1 gap at $5 - 0.4557 \times 4.68 = 2.8673$ nats.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:twoscalar} gives $0.4557 \le (G-\mu)/(G-2\eps)$; multiply by
$G-2\eps > 0$ and rearrange.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and applications}

\subsection{The screening pipeline}

Given a donor network, a held-out corpus of $N$ positions, a candidate block, and
a drift budget $\eps$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{One forward pass.} Record $u(x) \in \mathbb{R}^m$ for each position.
\item \textbf{Gaps.} Compute $\gap(x) = u(x)_{(1)} - u(x)_{(2)}$, the difference
  between the largest and second-largest score. Cost $O(Nm)$.
\item \textbf{Margin scarcity.} $\unc = \frac1N \#\{x : \gap(x) \le 2\eps\}$, plus
  any positions where the drift budget is known to be violated. Cost $O(N)$.
\item \textbf{Report.} $\damage \le \unc$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:screen}; accept the
  block at tolerance $\tau$ if $\unc \le \tau$, without transplanting.
\end{enumerate}
Total cost: one forward pass and $O(Nm)$ arithmetic, versus a full hybrid build
and evaluation per candidate block. Two degradations are available when even the
histogram is unwanted: replace step~3 by the collision-mass count
$\frac1N\#\{x : \sum_k u(x)_k^2 \le 4\eps^2\}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:difffrac}), or by
the two scalars $(G,\mu)$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:twoscalar}.

\subsection{Ranking blocks for merging}

For a stack of blocks, compute $\unc(b)$ for each and rank.
Theorem~\ref{thm:poscorr} says that if the screen's overshoot is at most $\eta$
and the damage spread across the stack exceeds $\eta/2$, the ranking is positively
correlated with the true damage ranking. Theorem~\ref{thm:opposite} says that
substituting weight-space distance can invert the ranking.

\subsection{Design guidance and auditing}

To make a block portable, make its decisions \emph{concentrated}: the block must
have low R\'enyi-2 entropy at most positions, or the margin route cannot certify
it at any drift level worth the name. Conversely, the diffuse tail of a
distribution is exactly where portability certificates are unobtainable, matching
the empirical observation that deep ``tail'' blocks are the fragile ones. Finally,
Theorem~\ref{thm:falsifiable} gives an audit: any claimed damage figure for a
transplant constrains the donor's mean gap, a consistency check costing one
forward pass.

\section{Discussion, limitations, future work}

\subsection{What survived and what failed}

\textbf{Survived.} The margin route is a genuine, certified screen.
Theorem~\ref{thm:screen} composes with Theorem~\ref{thm:revmarkov} into a
deployable two-scalar bound $\damage \le (G-\mu)/(G-2\eps)$, and with
Theorem~\ref{thm:difffrac} into an information-theoretic lower bound on the screen
itself. Across blocks the screen is provably positively correlated with damage
once its slack is smaller than twice the damage spread
(Theorems~\ref{thm:corr},~\ref{thm:poscorr}).

\textbf{Failed --- and the failure is a theorem.} The norm route survives only as
a \emph{sufficient} condition (Theorem~\ref{thm:norm-sound}). As a predictor it is
refuted by Theorems~\ref{thm:nonmono} and~\ref{thm:no-norm-bound}. Cross-block,
Theorem~\ref{thm:opposite} gives the same data set two opposite covariance signs.

\textbf{Needs a different definition.} ``Margin scarcity predicts damage'' cannot
be upgraded to an equality or a lower bound: Theorem~\ref{thm:conservative} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich-open} show the screen is one-sided by nature. Any
two-sided prediction must add information about \emph{where} the drift points
relative to the top-2 subspace.

\subsection{Limitations}

The linear block model is a stand-in for a genuine multi-layer block; the margin
results are statements about score fields and do not use it, but the
counterexamples are constructed inside it. The drift budget $\eps$ is an input,
and a mis-estimated $\eps$ invalidates the certificate. Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}
requires nonnegative score vectors, so for raw logits one must first pass through
a nonnegative representation. Theorem~\ref{thm:corr} assumes a uniform slack bound
$\eta$, which is not directly measurable without transplants, though it can be
estimated on a small calibration subset.

\subsection{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The top-2 projection is the complete predictor]
For a linear block there is a function of the projection of the weight drift onto
the per-position top-2 logit-difference direction that both upper- \emph{and}
lower-bounds the damage fraction, up to a universal constant. With
\[
s(x) = \bigl\langle \Delta W \cdot \feat(x),\; e_{d(x)} - e_{\mathrm{second}(x)}
\bigr\rangle
\]
and $\gap(x)$ the top-1 gap, one has
$\damage = \#\{x : s(x) > \gap(x)\}/\#\Omega$ exactly, and every norm-based or
margin-based statistic is a relaxation of this identity.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that all three counterexamples of this work --- dead direction,
live direction, diffuse-but-static block --- differ only in the angle between the
drift and the top-2 difference direction, and agree on everything the norm can
see.

Further directions: replace the hypothesis $|u-v| \le \eps$ by a computed bound,
closing the last non-certified input of the pipeline; extend the development from
argmax agreement to top-$k$ agreement or to a divergence between output
distributions, which requires replacing Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} by a stability
estimate for the whole simplex point; and sharpen the constant $\eta/2$ in
Theorem~\ref{thm:corr} under distributional assumptions on the damage profile
across blocks.

